appear from the 13th to the 21st century. From the 19th century, dry-farming areas shifted to Opuntia ficus-indica and then to citrus, a transition that accelerated after the Tajo\u2013Segura water transfer (post-1979), the most rapid landscape transformation in the area\u2019s history. The flora shows substantial turnover, including at least 11 high-confidence local extirpations, eight of them dating from the medieval period, and a sharp recent increase in naturalized exotic species (48 in 2026). Integrating multiple lines of evidence resolves apparent contradictions among datasets and offers a robust, temporally deep framework to inform heritage conservation and sustainable land management in Mediterranean landscapes.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.3390\/land15112115","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,11,6]],"date-time":"2026-11-06T09:23:23Z","timestamp":1793957003000},"page":"2115","update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/mdpi_crossmark_policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Multisecular Landscape Dynamics of a Medieval Islamic Royal Estate: Land-Use Change in Monteagudo-Cabezo de Torres (Murcia, Spain)"],"prefix":"10.3390","volume":"15","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6889-714X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Diego","family":"Rivera","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Departamento de Biolog\u00eda Vegetal, Facultad de Biolog\u00eda, Universidad de Murcia, Campus de Espinardo, 30100 Murcia, Spain"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3254-2691","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Francisco","family":"Alcaraz","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Departamento de Biolog\u00eda Vegetal, Facultad de Biolog\u00eda, Universidad de Murcia, Campus de Espinardo, 30100 Murcia, Spain"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4401-9884","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Javier