9"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["25GZZB09"]}]}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["Land"],"abstract":"<jats:p>Rapid urbanization concentrates infrastructure demand and places pressure on public services, environmental quality, and resilience. This study assesses provincial capacity to support green and low-carbon urban renewal in China. It examines how this capacity changes and relates to potential interprovincial connections. An index was constructed from 18 indicators across six dimensions for 30 provincial-level regions during 2020\u20132024. Dimensions received equal weights, with entropy weighting applied within each dimension. The analysis combined kernel-density estimation, spatial autocorrelation, a modified gravity model, social network analysis, and QAP regression. The national mean index increased from 0.222 to 0.360. Global Moran\u2019s I was insignificant in 2020 but significantly positive in 2021\u20132024. The network of model-derived potential associations was sparse, with distinct incoming, outgoing, and brokerage positions. Urbanization-stage, fiscal-autonomy, and construction-capacity differences, together with geographical adjacency, were positively associated with these modeled ties. Capacity rankings and modeled network positions provide complementary information for identifying relative renewal-capacity shortfalls and screening potential cooperation partners.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.3390\/land15112117","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,11,6]],"date-time":"2026-11-06T09:23:23Z","timestamp":1793957003000},"page":"2117","update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/mdpi_crossmark_policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Green and Low-Carbon Urban Renewal Capacity for Sustainable Infrastructure in China: A Spatial Network Analysis"],"prefix":"10.3390","volume":"15","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-9651-0472","authenticated-orcid":fals